    "description": "A rich, educative SpeakOut Kiddies resource on feelings, body signals, emotional expression, calming down, and asking for help. Includes lessons, examples, activities, worksheets, quiz, reflection, and facilitator guidance.",
    "contentHtml": "<article class=\"speakout-resource-book\">\n<h2>My Feelings</h2>\n<p><strong>Published by SpeakOut Mental Health Outreach.</strong> This resource helps children understand feelings, body signals, emotional expression, calming down, and asking for help in a simple, safe, practical, and age-appropriate way.</p>\n<h3>Learning Objectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Understand feelings, body signals, emotional expression, calming down, and asking for help.</li><li>Connect the lesson to real-life situations.</li><li>Practice using safe, kind, and responsible actions.</li><li>Reflect and ask for support when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>How to Use This Resource</h3>\n<p>This resource can be used at home, in school, or during a SpeakOut club session. Read one section at a time. Ask questions. Let the child speak, draw, act, write, count, observe, or demonstrate. The goal is not to rush. The goal is understanding, confidence, safety, and practical growth.</p>\n<h3>1. What This Means</h3>\n<p>My Feelings is not only something to read about. It is something children can practice in daily life. Children should understand the meaning, where they see it, why it matters, and how they can use it safely.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Understand the meaning.</li><li>Connect it to real life.</li><li>Practice one small step.</li><li>Ask for support when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Why It Matters</h3>\n<p>Children need practical life skills, not only academic information. When children understand this topic, they can participate better in class, relate better with friends, and make safer choices.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It builds confidence.</li><li>It supports emotional wellbeing.</li><li>It improves communication.</li><li>It helps children solve problems.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Real-Life Examples</h3>\n<p>A child can use this lesson at home, in school, with friends, and during personal reflection.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At home: practice during routines and conversations.</li><li>At school: use it during lessons and group work.</li><li>With friends: use it to share, listen, and solve problems.</li><li>Alone: use it to think, calm down, draw, read, or prepare.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Step-by-Step Practice</h3>\n<p>Children learn better when they know what to do next. This simple practice pathway helps the child move from knowledge to action.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pause and notice what is happening.</li><li>Name the issue or feeling.</li><li>Ask a clear question.</li><li>Try one safe action.</li><li>Reflect on what worked.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Practice Activities</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw a picture connected to My Feelings and explain it in three sentences.</li>\n<li>Act out a short scene showing the wrong way and the better way.</li>\n<li>Write or say three examples: one at home, one in school, and one with friends.</li>\n<li>Create a seven-day challenge and practice one small action daily.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Mini Worksheet</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Today’s topic is: My Feelings.</li>\n<li>One new word I learned: ______.</li>\n<li>One thing I understand better: ______.</li>\n<li>One question I still have: ______.</li>\n<li>One safe action I will practice: ______.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quick Quiz</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>What is one thing you learned about My Feelings?</li>\n<li>Where can you practice this lesson?</li>\n<li>Who can support you if you need help?</li>\n<li>What is one safe action you can take today?</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What did I learn today?</li>\n<li>What part was easy for me?</li>\n<li>What part do I need to practice again?</li>\n<li>How can I use this lesson at home, in school, or with friends?</li>\n<li>Who can support me if I need help?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Safety / Wellbeing Note</h3><p>If a child shares a safety concern, emotional distress, bullying, abuse, or thoughts of self-harm, take it seriously and seek appropriate safeguarding or professional support.</p>\n<h3>Parent Guide</h3>\n<p>Use this resource slowly. Children learn best when adults listen, ask open questions, and give practical examples. Avoid shame, comparison, shouting, or harsh correction. Praise effort, not only correct answers.</p>\n<h3>Teacher / Facilitator Guide</h3>\n<p>Use discussion, drawing, role-play, peer learning, and reflection. Keep the session respectful and safe.</p>\n<h3>SpeakOut Challenge</h3>\n<p>Practice one small action connected to My Feelings every day for one week.</p>\n<h3>SpeakOut Learning Promise</h3>\n<p>I can learn. I can ask questions. I can try again. I can speak kindly. I can ask for help. I can take one safe step today.</p>\n</article>",
